{
  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Los Angeles Dodgers were a team that looked, on paper, like a dynasty in full bloom. One hundred wins, 801 runs scored, a rotation anchored by Yoshinobu Yamamoto, and a lineup featuring Shohei Ohtani, Freddie Freeman, Mookie Betts, and the newly acquired Kyle Tucker. From the very first week of the season, Will Smith's opening-series home run and Max Muncy's early power surges signaled that this was a team built to dominate. The offense was deep, the pitching was elite, and Los Angeles looked every bit the favorite to make another deep October run.\n\nThe cracks, however, began to show in the bullpen. Tanner Scott, entrusted as a high-leverage weapon, became a recurring liability — surrendering critical home runs in late May and early June that kept opponents alive and cost the Dodgers winnable games. Edwin Díaz was no safer, imploding on back-to-back August nights against Arizona as Geraldo Perdomo and Corbin Carroll carved him up with extra-base hits. Alex Vesia added to the misery. What should have been a comfortable cushion in the NL West standings became a tense, exhausting grind, with the bullpen threatening to unravel what the starters and lineup had carefully built.\n\nThe offense responded with moments of genuine brilliance. Dalton Rushing's clutch late-game single in June, Teoscar Hernández's grand slam on the Fourth of July weekend, and a scorching late-summer stretch featuring Max Muncy, Tommy Edman, and Mookie Betts all reminded the baseball world just how dangerous this lineup could be. Ohtani remained a consistent force, and the team's run differential never wavered — they were genuinely one of baseball's best teams by every measure that counted.\n\nAnd yet, when September arrived and the standings were tallied, the Dodgers found themselves on the outside looking in. One hundred wins — a total that would earn a playoff berth in almost any other season — was not enough in the brutally competitive 2026 National League. The bullpen's accumulated damage, a handful of devastating late-inning losses, and the relentless pressure of a loaded division combined to deny Los Angeles a postseason berth. It stands as one of the cruelest outcomes in recent franchise history: a team with Hall of Fame talent, 100 victories, and an offense that averaged nearly five runs a game, watching October baseball from home.",
